"use strict";(self.webpackChunkspa_template=self.webpackChunkspa_template||[]).push([[30],{3030:s=>{s.exports=JSON.parse('{"created":"2018-03-25T10:29:00","modified":"2018-03-25T10:29:00","id":1038,"title":"ASP.Net Core Configuration\'s Strange Behavior\\n","article":"<p>For <a href=\\"http://asp.net/\\">ASP.Net</a> core they introduced an entirely new and self contained Configuration system, I wont go into the basics, but there is a good getting started article <a href=\\"https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/configuration/?tabs=basicconfiguration\\">here</a>.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"whats-the-issue\\">Whats the issue?</h2>\\n<p>Most people start with code similar to the following:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">class</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">Startup</span>\\n{\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">Startup</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\">IConfiguration configuration</span>)</span> =&gt; Configuration = configuration;\\n\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> IConfiguration Configuration { <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">get</span>; }\\n\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">void</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">ConfigureServices</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\">IServiceCollection services</span>)</span>\\n    {\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// setting up our services</span>\\n    }\\n}</code></pre>\\n<p>This gives us access to the configuration object that was created in Program.cs when we setup our service, which is great.</p>\\n<p>And most also have an app.settings.json file similar too:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-json\\">{\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">\\"MySettings\\"</span>: {\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">\\"SettingA\\"</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"Hello\\"</span>,\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">\\"SettingB\\"</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"World\\"</span>\\n    }\\n}</code></pre>\\n<p>The problem comes from how you access these values and when, in the code below we try and access them via GetSection which returns a <code>ConfigurationSection</code></p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\"><span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">void</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">ConfigureServices</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\">IServiceCollection services</span>)</span>\\n{\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> mySettings = Configuration.GetSection(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"MySettings\\"</span>);\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> settingB = Configuration.GetSection(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"MySettings:SettingB\\"</span>);\\n}</code></pre>\\n<p>GetSection always returns a <code>ConfigurationSection</code> object whether it finds a matching value or not, if nothing is found, then the <code>Value</code> property is null.</p>\\n<p>If you run the code above <code>mySettings</code> has a null value, suggesting the section doesnt exist at all, but <code>settingB</code> has the value <code>&quot;World&quot;</code> meaning that it does know about the section. Using <code>Configuration.GetValue&lt;T&gt;()</code> behaves the same as above too.</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> mySettings = Configuration.GetValue&lt;MySettings&gt;(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"MySettings\\"</span>);\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> settingB = Configuration.GetValue&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">string</span>&gt;(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"MySettings:SettingB\\"</span>);</code></pre>\\n<p>So can you not get a full &#39;section&#39;? Yes we can but we have to register it as a service itself. So it can be used throughout the application via Dependency Injection.</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\"><span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">public</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">void</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">ConfigureServices</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\">IServiceCollection services</span>)</span>\\n{\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// This makes the settings available to classes via IOptions</span>\\n    services.Configure&lt;MySettings&gt;(configuration.GetSection(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"MySettings\\"</span>));\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// This takes it a stage further and makes it directly availble as an injectable object</span>\\n    service.AddSingleton(c =&gt; cfg.GetService&lt;IOptions&lt;MyConfigOptions&gt;&gt;().Value);\\n}</code></pre>\\n<p>So all is good yeah? Well not quite, the section is still not accessible in the ConfigureServices function you are setting this up in. So you are reduced to accessing the values directly if needed.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"why-is-it-like-this\\">Why Is It Like This?</h2>\\n<p>Our settings come in from a variety of source types such as JSON, Environment Variable, XML, Command Line etc. To make all of these work together the Configuration parsers for each type flatten the key/values into Dictionary objects. So in the example above the Dictionary would contain.</p>\\n<pre><code>&quot;MySettings:SettingA&quot; = &quot;Hello&quot;\\n&quot;MySettings:SectionB&quot; = &quot;World&quot;  </code></pre>\\n<p>This allows the different types of configuration to be able to override each other with a &#39;last configuration wins&#39; approach.</p>\\n<p>When we register the section in the services with the <code>Configure</code> method, it uses the <code>ConfigrationSection</code> and the other properties (other than <code>Value</code>) to returned to extract anything it can Dictionary into an object, which you can then regsiter as a value.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"additional\\">Additional</h2>\\n<p>FabianGosebrink in this reply on <a href=\\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/46019014/653458\\">StackOverflow</a> explains this really well and suggests a cool extension method.</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\">public static class IServiceCollectionExtensions\\n{\\n    public static IServiceCollection AddConfigOptions&lt;TOptions&gt;(this IServiceCollection services, IConfiguration configuration, string section) where TOptions : class, new()\\n    {\\n        services.Configure&lt;TOptions&gt;(configuration.GetSection(section));\\n        services.AddSingleton(cfg =&gt; cfg.GetService&lt;IOptions&lt;TOptions&gt;&gt;().Value);\\n\\n        return services;\\n    }\\n}</code></pre>\\n<p>Usage:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\">services.AddConfigOptions&lt;MySettings&gt;(Configuration, <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"MySettings\\"</span>);</code></pre>\\n<h3 id=\\"references\\">References</h3>\\n<ul>\\n<li><a href=\\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/46019014/653458\\">https://stackoverflow.com/a/46019014/653458</a></li>\\n<li><a href=\\"https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/configuration/?tabs=basicconfiguration\\">https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/configuration/?tabs=basicconfiguration</a></li>\\n<li><a href=\\"https://weblog.west-wind.com/posts/2017/Dec/12/Easy-Configuration-Binding-in-ASPNET-Core-revisited\\">https://weblog.west-wind.com/posts/2017/Dec/12/Easy-Configuration-Binding-in-ASPNET-Core-revisited</a></li>\\n</ul>\\n","showInResults":true,"tags":[]}')}}]);